// THE LAND of the digging wheel. One skin laid in rings about the machine's turning axis (the world's y axis): the pit's
// bench, three terraces each cut to the wheel's own round (a quarter pipe of the wheel's radius, less what has broken out of
// it where the flight is near: scars along the beds, lumps left standing, a ragged crest, fallen earth banked along each
// foot; the teeth's scores are drawn by the footprint), the present cut front at the wheel, the lip with its topsoil and the crop's cut edge, and beyond the lip the field: level
// north and south of the pit, rising to the east as THE HILL, rolling far out to a horizon ring. To the south the bay flares
// open; THE PIT IS CLOSED by its far sides (PIT): terraced faces with the same beds, broad old berms, some 630 m west of the
// axis, 440 m south, 150 m north, the land rising behind them with fields on top. THE PIT FLOOR is brown earth, darker than
// the faces: the six crawlers' prints where the machine came in (CRAWL), the haul road with its berms (ROAD: round three b,
// the road no pale strip but the floor's own earth packed, its berms and the windrows low broken banks of that earth), windrows,
// tipped heaps (MOUNDS), boulders (BOULDERS), rows of old spoil across the floor to the west (ridges()), stones and clods
// lying as bodies where the lens is near (pitLumps: in drifts, a fist to a boulder, many half sunk), a darker damp line
// here and there in a rut. THE PIT HOLDS NO WATER (round three b: the three pools are taken out, with their mirror, their
// shore's bright line and their ripples; POOLS is an empty list). The floor's surface is a
// height law lit by the low sun from its own slope (cleats in the prints, ruts, cracks, the scraper's rake, clods). The clouds'
// shadows drift over the whole pit on the clock (pitCloud).
// THE FIELD IS RIPE BARLEY standing to the lip: far off a coat drawn in the land's shader (wind bands on the clock, tramlines,
// the ears' speckle and sheen); by the lens's line over the land and along the bay's lip REAL STANDING CROP (buildCrop:
// upright ribbons along the drill rows over level sheets of ears, stalk and bearded head drawn in each ribbon by the pixel's
// footprint; in the sheets the ears as heads of grain IN THE DRILL ROWS, each nodding downwind but turned its own way;
// and where the lens is low over the grain REAL EARS, each a bearded head of its own that goes with the wind: EARS).
// THE DRILL ROWS SHOW AS ROWS where the eye looks along them, the ears high on each row's line and the stand's dark
// between two rows (tufts, fd_rows), gone to their mean when the footprint across them outgrows them; a row's ribbon
// sinks into the stand by the footprint. LODGED PATCHES lie in the field (fd_lodge); no flower stands in it (round
// three b). AT THE LIP THE CROP ENDS IN BROKEN EARTH (round three, three b): south of the cut front the topsoil has
// caved back (lipBite: round scallops and slips, never steps), by the wheel in a hollow of its own whose floor is the
// bare loam and whose back is a scarp of dark topsoil over pale loam; behind the caved edge, as far round as 1.0 of a
// radian, lies A SHOULDER OF BARE EARTH four to nearly ten metres broad (lipBare; round three d: it was two to five, which the stand hid from the look back), stripped
// and trodden, lit as earth; the crop stands level to its last stalk behind that, on a line of its own (cropBack),
// and ends in upright stalks; DARK CLODS AND NOTHING ELSE lie on the shoulder, on the floor of the wheel's bite, on
// the ledge and on the terrace below (buildClods). NORTH OF THE SHELTER ROW, AND EAST OF ITS END DOWN TO THE LANE, THE HOME FIELD IS CUT as far east as x 174 (the core's HARVEST;
// cutD; the way out flies over it): stubble in the land's shader, the straw in windrows (SWATH, buildSwaths), the standing barley ending at the
// cut's line in a straight wall of stalks. AT THE LANE the stand comes
// down to the verge through shorter stalks, a furrow of bare earth and rough grass, along an edge that wanders.
// The faces show their beds (unbroken: no fault throws them, round three b), rills and fallen earth (heaps of talus as real geometry where the flight is
// near); the fresh cut in the socket is damp earth, darker than the dried faces beside it; in the socket the two lower
// benches' crests have slipped (crestSlip), and what went lies in lumps below. On the land: the lane (in the shader, cut off at
// the lip), the house, the stone barn, the yard's wall, two poles (which are FADED OUT by the pixel's footprint while
// still broader than one, named below as a fault of its own), THE FIELD FENCE that the cut has reached (FENCE: five posts and three wires in a row
// across the bare shoulder to the torn edge by the wheel's bite, none in the barley, three posts more gone over the edge; ITS WIRE
// IS A REAL SIX-SIDED TUBE, 3 cm across, built on the wire's own line (polish3), and the light reads its faces: the sun
// on the side turned to it, the side turned away dark, the top of a tube where the lens looks down on it. THE FACES ARE
// READ EVERYWHERE ON THIS FLIGHT AND WHAT IS NOT REACHED IS ONE FACE FILLING A PIXEL BY ITSELF: a tube 3 cm thick is
// finer than a pixel at every metre of the lens's line at 800 by 450, so what the picture holds everywhere on this line
// is the mean of the faces the lens sees, each in the share of the breadth it shows, and the tubes themselves - 72 of the fence's 144 triangles - put no pixel
// on the screen; the tube's own faces begin to carry the wire only nearer than 10 to 11 m and carry it alone inside 3
// to 4 m, and the line comes no nearer a wire than 14.2 m (metre 236.9 of 455.1, u 0.521). IT WAS A THIN LIMB
// painted down the middle of A FLAT UPRIGHT BAND, and what was drawn of it fell by the cosine of the sight line's
// angle from level (0.71 at 45 degrees, nothing from straight overhead) and was gone altogether before the pixel
// outgrew the band. That cosine read nothing of the light: a flat upright strap's breadth on the picture simply falls
// away as it is seen from above, and the term faded the wire out with it, to cover the body the strap did not have -
// a strap has no top to show from overhead. The tube is that body, and nothing is faded now for want of one: what is
// drawn of the wire is its own share of the pixel, the breadth the tube shows across the sight line against the
// pixel's own footprint there, with the ground behind it in the rest of the pixel - a pixel broad at the least, and
// fainter in step as the tube grows finer. The tube is three quarters of a pixel where the line passes nearest and a
// twentieth of one at the entry ring, 215 m off, and a bar that fine, drawn alone, is caught only by the pixels whose
// middles happen to fall on it and comes out as dots with bare ground between them; so an open sleeve 0.3 m broad is
// laid about the tube to catch the pixel for it, which is no part of the wire and shows nothing of itself. THE
// SLEEVE'S REACH IS A LIMIT AND IS NAMED AS ONE: it is a pixel broad or more within 97 to 112 m, which on this line is
// from metre 134 of 455 to the end, and farther off than that the wire is caught again only where a pixel's middle
// falls on the sleeve and reads as a thin broken thread, a twentieth of the wire's own weight at the entry ring.
// Nothing grows the sleeve with the distance, for that would be a read of the lens's distance, which this world has
// not got), four poplars that sway and stir on the clock; THE SHELTER ROW, eleven old limes along the yard's
// north side (LIMES) with a hedge at their foot (ROWHEDGE), which stand where they cover the exit ring with its glow
// from the pit and from the lip: from no metre of the lens's line before metre 322, at any of the four windows, is a
// point of the ring in sight past the limes' closed bodies and the hedge's; and north
// and north-east of the exit ring, where the way out looks: eighteen full-grown oaks (OAKS: seven in the old north hedge, five in the pasture beyond it), the home field's own hedges
// as real hedgerows (HEDGES), pasture beyond them, and the crest wood's canopy (WOOD) on the hill's shoulder.
// Nothing here reads a seed: every count and every place is the same on every visit. All that moves is a function of uT.
// WHEN THIS FILE IS READ its typed figures are set out and a few dozen sums on typed figures are worked, no more: the
// field's rings' spacing (FIELD), the lens's line over the field and its length (FLY, FLY_K), the flare's constant
// (FLARE_K), the boulders' places by the road (BOULDERS). No loop goes over the land, nothing is built, nothing is
// measured: the heaps, the ridges, the boulders' lumps and their shadows' bars, the shaders' shadow lists and
// LAND_BODIES are worked when a build (or a script) first asks for them.
// THE LENS IN THESE SHADERS. Its place is read for three things only, as light itself needs it: the air between (the core's
// haze, and the far country's own thinner haze); the side of a face that is seen (the core's faceNormal); and THE DIRECTION
// OF SIGHT, never the distance, in the ears' sheen
// and the stand's own shade where the lens looks toward the sun (cropLit), in the field fence's wire, where the
// breadth its tube shows ACROSS the sight line and the faces of it the lens sees are what one pixel holds of it, and
// the sine between that bearing and the wire's own line foreshortens the wire's length and so gives the pixel's
// footprint across it (polish3), and in the light that comes through leaves
// (round three b: no pool's mirror and no shore's bright line read it any more: the pit floor's shader reads the lens for the haze alone).
// ROUND THREE E: A BROADLEAF IS BUILT OF SPRAYS (trSpray) and its outline is their tips. THE READS OF ROUNDS THREE B
// TO D ARE GONE WITH THE MASSES OF LEAF: no skin is thrown away by the renderer's facing (gl_FrontFacing is read
// nowhere in this file's leaf), NO OUTLINE IS BITTEN by the angle between a mass's round and the direction of sight,
// and no footprint is taken the narrow way (tc_narrow). (The world file's head says the same.) A spray's leaf reads: the pixel's footprint (its rim is frayed into leaf near
// to, the leaf's grain on it: each goes to its own mean as the footprint outgrows it, the fray by its noise going to one half against the same threshold, so that a spray is no broader from far; the row's ragged foot);
// the flat face as the lens sees it (the core's faceNormal: a facet's own light, its under side darker); the
// direction of sight in the light that comes through leaves; the air between.
// THE LENS IS NEVER READ TO PLACE A THING, NOR TO SHOW OR HIDE ONE FOR WANT OF A BODY; where the lens enters a drawing
// it enters as light does (the air between, the side of a face that is seen, the bearing of sight). What fades with
// fineness (every fine pattern, the sprays' frayed rims and their leaf, the rough grass's tussocks, the ragged foot of
// the row) fades by the pixel's footprint, and goes to its own mean as it grows too fine to draw.
// (THE FENCE'S WIRE IS NO LONGER FADED BY THE EYE'S HEIGHT, nor for want of a body. It was: it was a flat upright band
// with the wire painted down its middle, read off the metres across the band that each corner carried, and because a
// flat upright strap's breadth on the picture falls away as it is seen from above, what was drawn of the wire fell with
// it by the cosine of the sight line's angle from level, to nothing from straight overhead. That term read nothing of
// the light; it faded the wire away to cover the body the strap did not have. Polish3 gave the wire a six-sided tube of
// its own, 3 cm across, and what is drawn of it is that body's own share of the pixel: the breadth the tube shows
// across the sight line against the pixel's footprint there. It is a pixel broad at the least and grows fainter in step
// as the tube grows finer, which is the lawful filtering, and it is never nothing while the sleeve that catches the
// pixel for it is a pixel broad (within 97 to 112 m; beyond that the thread breaks, which is named at the place and in
// the sentence above). ONE SAVE, TRUE OF THE CODE AND NOT REACHED ON THIS FLIGHT: with the sight line swung within a
// ten-thousandth of a radian of the wire's own line what is drawn of it still falls toward nothing, which is named
// UNMENDED at the place, with the honest mend. THE LINE AND THE LENGTH OF WIRE ONE PIXEL SPANS ARE THE PLACE'S OWN DERIVATIVES, taken from the
// change, one pixel to the next, of the place on the wire's own line that each corner carries; THE OFFSET AND THE
// FOOTPRINT ARE THOSE TWO TAKEN ACROSS THE SIGHT LINE, which is the lens's bearing and nothing else - no depth and no
// distance from the lens, except the haze, which reads the distance as the air between does, and no cosine of the
// eye's height anywhere in it.)
// (THE TWO POLES BY THE LANE STILL FADE TO NOTHING, and under this world's own rule of reading that is a fault, not
// lawful filtering: what is drawn of a pole is whole to a footprint of 0.15 m and gone by 0.35 m (FARM_FRAG, the pole's
// own line), so the pole is DELETED - not let go to the mean of the pole and the sky behind it over the pixel - and
// deleted while it is still broader than a pixel, for a pole is 0.4 m thick and at a footprint of 0.35 m it still shows
// 1.1 pixels (1.14 across the corners of its eight sides, 1.06 across their flats); at the fade's start, a footprint
// of 0.15 m, it shows 2.5 to 2.7. (Those two footprints are some 130 m and 56 m
// off at 800 by 450, taking a pixel's breadth as the plain distance over 374.4; the pole's own foot is footprint(vW),
// which is that breadth on a face square to the sight line and more on one turned away, so the fade ends sooner.) It is a fault of the same shape
// as the wire's old band: a thing faded away rather than carried honestly, though here it is the fineness and not the
// eye's height that fades it. It is named here UNMENDED: polish3 was given the wire alone and did not touch the poles'
// code. THE HONEST MEND IS THE WIRE'S OWN, and the pole needs no new body for it - it is already a real eight-sided
// round 0.4 m thick (buildPole, Geo.cyl) with its own faces: draw its own share of the pixel, the breadth it shows
// across the sight line against the footprint, with what stands behind it in the rest of the pixel, and lay about it
// the same kind of open sleeve to catch the pixel where it is finer than one, which is beyond 150 m.)
// A flat face's normal and the footprint across the drill rows (tuftFoot) are taken across neighbouring pixels.
//
// WHAT THE OTHER FILES USE:
//   landY(x, z)        the land's height over (x, z): the mesh's own everywhere near the flight and the bodies (heaps of talus,
//                      tipped heaps, berms, windrows and the old spoil's rows too; not the boulders, which are bodies)
//   fieldY(x, z)       the uncut land's height (the hill, the yard, the far country) whether or not the pit has taken it
//   lipR(th), flare(th), wide(th), outline(th), CUT, LIP, PIT, HOUSE, BARN, YARDW, POPLARS, POPLAR, POLES, POLE, LANE, YARD, HILL,
//   CROP, EARS, FLY (the core's ROUTE where it goes over the field), CRAWL, ROAD, MOUNDS, POOLS, BOULDERS, ridges(), OAKS, HEDGES, HEDGE, WOOD, woodEdge(z),
//   LIMES, limeTrees(), ROWHEDGE, rowHedge(), lipBite(th) (how far the topsoil's edge is torn back from lipR), lipBare(th) (the bare
//   shoulder's breadth behind it), cropBack(th) (how far behind lipR the crop's last stalks stand), lipSag(th), SWATH, cutD(x, z), buildSwaths(G), CLOD_CLEAR
//   BEDS, bedHAt(x, y, z)   the beds of earth the faces are cut through (each its top, colour, how it stands and how firm it is) and the beds' own height at a place
//   LAND_BODIES        the standing bodies as simple shapes (boxes and round bars) for the clear-air script: the farmstead
//                      (house, barn, wall, the gate's two piers), the poles, the field fence ('fence', its standing row; 'fence, fallen'), the poplars, the oaks, the hedgerows and the crest
//                      wood (names 'oak', 'hedge', 'wood'), the shelter row's limes (each twice: 'yard lime', its closed
//                      body; 'yard lime crown', its leaves' whole reach), the hedge at the row's foot (boxes inside its
//                      closed core: 'yard thorn'), and on the pit floor the tipped heaps, the boulders, the berms,
//                      windrows and spoil. An array, read as one (length, a walk, map); it is worked at its first reading
//                      (landBodies() gives the same list afresh)
//   buildLand(ctx, uniforms) -> { mesh, things, triangles, counts }   (async; things is a group: the farmstead and trees, the
//                      poles (a mesh of their own, which blends), the crop; of the sun's shadow table it fills ONE BOX (the
//                      house's walls) and EIGHT of the land's ten CAPSULES (the house's roof, the barn, two poles, four
//                      poplars' crowns); the boulders', the tipped heaps', the near berms' and windrows', the oaks' and
//                      the poplars' stems' and crowns' feet's shadows are tried in the land's own shader, not through the
//                      table; the limes' in every shader of this file that asks for the sun on the land (rowCut))
