// WHERE A FACE STANDS AGAINST ITS ROUND at a place on it (fi: the terrace; s: the metres along the sweep; u: the metres up
// the round; hb: the beds' height there). A CUT FACE IS THE WHEEL'S OWN ROUND LESS WHAT HAS BROKEN OUT OF IT: the stiff
// clay stands at the round; marl, loam, sand and the seam have ravelled back from it, each by its own measure (BEDS.out
// less the clay's); and where a lump has come away there is a sharp-rimmed scar, longer along the bed than it is high:
// up to 0.8 m deep in a loose bed, 0.45 m in a firm one. Gives [the metres it stands proud (never more than 0.1 m;
// to 1.4 m behind), the scar's share 0..1].
// (faceNoise: the three chances of a place on a face, which do not ask the bed: where a scar lies, the small unevenness, how
